 @SKHYJINXBenchmarks  Hey, it's actually a decent option if you just want AV1 encode/decode now and don't feel like paying >$850. It's going to take another year for Nvidia or AMD to filter that down the product stack if Nvidia's CES slides are anything to go by.

But for gaming? Meh. That class of cards is having a lot of trouble doing modern (2021+) titles at 1080p60 low. Everyone's going to have to step up their game or offer some serious discounts to make many of them a decent buy. Ifyou're looking a "new" (as in currently manufactured) sub-$200 graphics cards it's okay given the other options. If it wasn't for that dumb decision to require PCIe power and requiring ReBar, I'd almost recommend it over the 1650 non-Super/Ti. You can rip a lot of performance out of it with a sizeable OC and beat a 1650 in most cases. The drivers are improving by quite a bit, but it's probably going to be a beta test until Battlemage. Buying Arc is gambling on whether or not Intel is going to stick with it.

But in a world where the used market (1070/2060/RX 5(5/6)00) and the RX 6600 exists skirting that price point, they're all a hard pass unless you really want/need a single-slot, low-profile, slot power only GPU or some other niche purpose.

